// **Querying a file's noted declarations** (ADR-0100): `noted_count("x")` and `noted_name("x", i)`. W6's third
// sub-wave, and the half a build script actually needs.
//
// ADR-0099 gave notes a **reader**: a script could ask a *named* declaration about its notes. Its closing
// consequence was exact about what was missing — a script had to name each declaration, so it could not ask
// "every declaration tagged `@X`". This is that question, for a script written against a note it will meet in
// code it has not seen.
//
// **The honest limit, stated here because it is the interesting part.** A folding intrinsic is answered at
// *check* time, so every argument must be readable then — and a `for` variable is not, because it exists only
// at run time. So this does **not** work, and no spelling makes it work:
//
//     for i: 0..noted_count("serialise") { name := noted_name("serialise", i); … }
//
// Genuine loop-driven iteration needs the query to lower to *real code* reading a **compiler-emitted table** —
// static data the back end emits and the VM can also read. Jairs has no such mechanism: it is the same one
// `Type_Info`'s variable-length field list has been deferred for since ADR-0078, and it is owed its own wave.
// So after this sub-wave notes can be **counted** and **named**, and cannot be **looped over**. That is the
// boundary of folding rather than a gap in the syntax, and saying so is better than implying the message loop
// is nearly finished.
//
// What is checked here:
//
//   * `noted_count` over a file with **two** `@serialise` procedures and one without — so the count is a real
//     count rather than "any" or "all";
//   * `noted_name` at index 0 and 1, in **declaration order**: the one order a reader can predict from the
//     source. Sorting by name would make *inserting* a declaration renumber every index a script had
//     unrolled, and a hash order would make one program answer differently between runs;
//   * an **out-of-range** index answering `""` rather than being refused, because unrolling to a fixed bound
//     is the intended use and its tail must be quiet — a script written for "up to four serialisable types"
//     has to compile in a file with two;
//   * a **different** note (`@internal`) counted separately, so the query is per-note and not per-file;
//   * a note on a `#expand` macro counted alongside an ordinary procedure, since notes belong to the
//     declaration rather than to how it is called.
//
// This file's exit code depends on **every** answer: it is 211 (2 declarations × 100, then a 1-byte first
// name × 10, then a 1-byte second name, then 0 for the out-of-range read). Making the query return nothing
// moves it to 255, which is what makes the answers load-bearing rather than decorative — the corpus
// differential compares the two engines to each other, so a wrong answer both give is invisible.
//
// The query sees **this file only**: a note in an imported module is not counted. That is the same boundary a
// cross-file `#expand` splice has (E0272) and a cross-file instantiation has, and for the same reason —
// reaching across files during checking is what makes sema and the module loader mutually recursive, PLAN §5's
// named top risk. A build script is a file, so the boundary is where a build script would want it.
#import "Basic";

/// The first `@serialise`, so index 0 names this one. A single-letter name, because the exit code compares
/// name *lengths* — comparing text needs a byte loop, which is `String`'s job in W7 (E0278, ADR-0099 §4).
a :: (x: s64) -> s64 @serialise {
    return x;
}

/// Not noted at all, so it is skipped rather than counted — sitting *between* the two that are counted, so
/// declaration order is genuinely tested rather than accidentally satisfied.
b :: (x: s64) -> s64 {
    return x;
}

/// The second `@serialise`. A macro, since a note belongs to the declaration rather than to how it is called.
c :: (x: s64) -> s64 #expand @serialise {
    return x + x;
}

/// A different note, counted on its own — the query is per-note.
d :: (x: s64) -> s64 @internal {
    return x;
}

main :: () {
    // Two `@serialise`s: `a` and `c`, with `b` skipped between them.
    n := noted_count("serialise");

    // Declaration order, so these are `a` then `c` — one byte each.
    first := noted_name("serialise", 0);
    second := noted_name("serialise", 1);

    // Past the end. `""` rather than a refusal, so an unrolled tail is quiet.
    past := noted_name("serialise", 2);

    // A different note, counted separately: one `@internal`.
    internal := noted_count("internal");

    // A note nothing carries. Zero is a real answer, not a sentinel.
    missing := noted_count("nosuchnote");

    exit(n * 100 + first.count * 10 + second.count + past.count + internal - 1 + missing);
}
